WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.213
Vision-language-action models inherit rich semantics from large-scale pretraining.

00:00:06.263 --> 00:00:09.993
But they capture limited metric geometry from monocular images,

00:00:09.993 --> 00:00:13.071
which hinders precise and contact-rich manipulation.

00:00:14.121 --> 00:00:17.213
How can a pretrained VLA use stereo geometry

00:00:17.213 --> 00:00:20.375
and its uncertainty for precise manipulation?

00:00:22.425 --> 00:00:26.053
We present StereoLift: enhancing vision-language-action

00:00:26.053 --> 00:00:29.417
models for precise manipulation via stereo lifting.

00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:35.911
StereoLift is one interface between a rectified stereo pair and a pretrained VLA.

00:00:35.911 --> 00:00:40.063
The stereo pair, a language instruction and the robot state go in.

00:00:40.063 --> 00:00:42.138
A 50-step action chunk comes out.

00:00:43.338 --> 00:00:45.929
First, a frozen stereo foundation model

00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:49.052
returns disparity together with its confidence.

00:00:49.402 --> 00:00:54.885
Second, a geometric token embedding lifts each visual token to 3D with assumed intrinsics

00:00:54.885 --> 00:00:57.966
and adds it through a zero-initialized projection.

00:00:57.966 --> 00:01:00.492
The policy receives no camera parameters.

00:01:01.692 --> 00:01:05.716
Third, 16 relation tokens, conditioned on language and robot state,

00:01:05.716 --> 00:01:09.920
read hand-object relations at the native resolution of the belief map.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.562
Cells with low confidence are down-weighted.

00:01:13.762 --> 00:01:15.792
The pretrained VLA stays as it is.

00:01:15.792 --> 00:01:19.375
PaliGemma and the flow-matching action expert are unchanged,

00:01:19.375 --> 00:01:22.122
and training uses only the flow-matching loss.

00:01:23.122 --> 00:01:26.390
We deploy StereoLift on a Unitree G1-D humanoid

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.727
with two dexterous hands and evaluate six tasks.

00:01:29.727 --> 00:01:34.525
Every clip is a complete rollout of the deployed policy, at 6x speed.

00:01:35.325 --> 00:01:36.223
InsertRing.

00:01:36.223 --> 00:01:41.777
StereoLift succeeds in 16 of 20 trials, against 8 for monocular RGB.

00:01:42.558 --> 00:01:45.157
StackCup: three-cup stacking.

00:01:45.157 --> 00:01:46.949
15 of 20, against 9.

00:01:53.558 --> 00:01:56.638
RemoveCap: take the cap off a bottle.

00:01:56.638 --> 00:01:58.302
19 of 20, against 7.

00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:06.689
BuildBlocks, a bimanual assembly.

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:11.918
Lift a base brick, insert three blocks into it, and place the assembly.

00:02:11.918 --> 00:02:14.717
StereoLift succeeds in 8 of 20 trials.

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:16.337
Monocular RGB in none.

00:02:17.325 --> 00:02:18.716
HandoverBanana.

00:02:18.716 --> 00:02:20.572
20 of 20, against 9.

00:02:22.525 --> 00:02:26.119
DrawerItem, a long-horizon task with occlusion.

00:02:26.119 --> 00:02:27.649
20 of 20, against 7.

00:02:29.992 --> 00:02:34.097
StereoLift is the best method on all six tasks.

00:02:34.097 --> 00:02:40.036
Its average success is 81.7%, compared with 33.3% for monocular RGB.

00:02:41.036 --> 00:02:47.028
In simulation, under the official RoboCasa365 Target50 protocol,

00:02:47.028 --> 00:02:53.955
StereoLift raises the overall success of the base VLA from 16.9% to 34.5%.

00:02:55.055 --> 00:02:58.720
On RoboCasa365, on Kitchen-18 and on the humanoid,

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.630
StereoLift achieves the highest success among the compared methods.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:07.193
It adds 31.4 ms per action chunk.

00:03:07.543 --> 00:03:14.690
Under target depth shifts of ±20 cm, it keeps 61.7% success on the humanoid.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.006
And with only 30 demonstrations per task on Kitchen-18,

00:03:19.006 --> 00:03:22.467
it still surpasses monocular RGB trained on 100.

00:03:24.867 --> 00:03:30.070
Code, the Kitchen-18 dataset and more results are available on our project page.
